					<h2>Maritime cybersecurity updates for this week:</h2>
<h3>November 7, 2025</h3>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>▶️ Cybercriminals Infiltrate Logistics and Freight Networks via Legitimate RMM Tools to Steal Cargo</strong></p>
<p>A sophisticated wave of attacks is targeting logistics and freight networks, focusing on the theft of physical cargo through digital means. The attack chain involves compromising accounts and using malicious URLs in emails to deploy legitimate RMM tools like ScreenConnect, SimpleHelp, PDQ Connect, and N-able, which grant the attackers persistent remote access. Once inside, they use credential harvesting tools such as WebBrowserPassView and, in at least one case, have deleted bookings and taken over dispatch communications to coordinate the theft of high-value loads like food and beverage products.</p>
<p><strong>▶️ Manufacturing and Transportation Lead as Top IoT Targets: Zscaler Uncovers Alarming Trends in Mobile and OT Attacks</strong></p>
<p>The Zscaler ThreatLabz 2025 Mobile, IoT, and OT Threat Report reveals a dramatic $387\%$ year-over-year increase in attacks targeting critical infrastructure within the energy sector, underscoring an escalating threat to essential industries. Furthermore, the Manufacturing and Transportation sectors now each account for $20.2\%$ of all observed IoT malware attacks, collectively representing over $40\%$ of incidents, with malware families like Mirai and Mozi leading the charge against these high-dependency industries.</p>
<p><strong>▶️ New Data Highlights Maritime Supply Chain Risk: Half of Suppliers Lack Key Security and ESG Policies</strong></p>
<p>The maritime industry&#8217;s supply chain is facing increased scrutiny, as a recent assessment found significant gaps in supplier compliance and security. Beyond the $28\%$ of suppliers flagged for high cyber risk, the review also showed that over half of suppliers lack information-security policies or third-party-assured anti-bribery systems. This signals a shift where cyber and ESG due diligence are becoming central to shipowners&#8217; procurement strategies, forcing a move from reactive to data-driven, predictive risk management.</p>
<p><strong>▶️ Hitachi Energy TropOS Routers Vulnerable to Remote OS Command Injection and Privilege Escalation</strong></p>
<p>Hitachi Energy has released an urgent update to patch critical vulnerabilities (CVSS v4 8.7) in its TropOS 4th Gen wireless mesh networking platform, which is widely used in ports, offshore platforms, and vessel-to-shore communications for OT/SCADA backbones. The flaws, which include OS Command Injection (CVE-2025-1036) and Improper Privilege Management (CVE-2025-1037), could allow an authenticated user with low privileges to execute arbitrary commands, gain root SSH access to the device, and potentially affect critical wireless OT communications. Users are strongly urged to update to firmware version 8.9.7.0 immediately.</p>
<p><strong>▶️ Fuji Electric HMI Software Vulnerable to Remote Code Execution via Malicious Project Files</strong></p>
<p>Fuji Electric has released an update to address high-severity buffer overflow vulnerabilities in its Monitouch V-SFT-6 HMI configuration software, which is widely used in marine engine rooms, cargo handling systems, and port machinery. The flaws, including Heap-based (CVE-2025-54496) and Stack-based (CVE-2025-54526) Buffer Overflows, are both scored at CVSS v4 8.4. Although not exploitable remotely, these vulnerabilities can be triggered by processing a specially crafted project file. Successful exploitation may allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code, potentially leading to a device crash or tampering with HMI logic, directly affecting OT safety. Fuji Electric recommends users update their software to version V6.2.9.0 or newer.</p>
<p><strong>▶️  Survision LPR Cameras Missing Default Authentication, Threatening Port Access Control</strong></p>
<p>A critical vulnerability (CVSS v4 9.3) in all versions of the Survision License Plate Recognition (LPR) Camera system allows for full system access without requiring any authentication. Since these cameras are essential for port gate automation, terminal access control, and customs inspection lanes, this flaw (CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function) could allow attackers to bypass physical access controls or tamper with surveillance data. Users should immediately update to firmware version v3.5 or newer and activate password authentication.</p>
<p><strong>▶️ Delta Electronics PLC/CNC Software Vulnerable to Code Execution via Malicious Files </strong></p>
<p>A high-severity Stack-based Buffer Overflow (CVE-2025-58317, CVSS v4 8.5) has been identified in Delta Electronics CNCSoft-G2 software, which is widely used for PLC and CNC configuration in shipyard manufacturing, crane controls, and port machinery. This flaw allows an attacker to execute arbitrary code in the context of the current process if a user is tricked into opening a malicious file. Successful exploitation poses a direct OT system risk, potentially leading to the remote manipulation of PLC logic or parameters. Users must update to Version 2.1.0.34 or later immediately.</p>
<p><strong>▶️ Vulnerability in ICM Viewer Client Component Could Allow Manipulation of IDIS Surveillance Systems</strong></p>
<p>A critical Argument Injection vulnerability (CVE-2025-12556, CVSS v4 8.7) has been found in IDIS ICM Viewer client software, which is used for monitoring IDIS CCTV/VMS systems widely installed in ports, terminals, and large vessels. The flaw is remotely exploitable with low attack complexity and could allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code within the context of the host machine. IDIS requires all users to immediately upgrade to version v1.7.1 or uninstall the software, as failure to update will render the ICM Viewer unusable.</p>
<p><strong>▶️ Rhysida Ransomware Gang Abuses Microsoft&#8217;s Trusted Signing to Distribute Malware via Fake Search Ads</strong></p>
<p>Threat actors are leveraging a sophisticated two-pronged strategy to evade detection: packaging the malware to obfuscate its function for a low static detection rate, and using code-signing certificates via an abuse of Microsoft&#8217;s Trusted Signing system. These short-lived, trusted certificates (valid for 72 hours) allow the group to sign files at scale, granting the malware automatic trust inside Windows and security tools, which complicates forensic investigations and delays defenders&#8217; response time. The focus for defenders must now shift to behavioral analytics and validating the source of the installer.</p>
<p><strong>▶️ Unauthenticated RCE in CWP and File Disclosure in Gladinet: CISA Mandates Federal Patching by November 25</strong></p>
<p>In addition to the CWP and Gladinet flaws, CISA&#8217;s alert follows reports of active exploitation against critical security vulnerabilities impacting three popular WordPress plugins and themes, all scored with a CVSS of 9.8. These flaws allow for severe actions, including unauthenticated privilege escalation in WP Freeio, authentication bypass in Noo JobMonster, and lack of authorization checks in Post SMTP that allows for administrator password changes and site takeover. Users of these plugins are urgently recommended to update them to the latest version.</p>
<p><strong>▶️ Canadian Cyber Centre Warns of Rising Hacktivist Attacks Exploiting Internet-Exposed ICS Devices</strong></p>
<p>The Canadian government agency provided specific examples of attacks impacting operations, including an intrusion at a water utility where hackers tampered with water pressure. In another incident reported by the Canadian Centre for Cyber Security, attackers accessed an oil and gas company&#8217;s automated tank gauge (ATG) and manipulated its values, triggering false alarms. A third incident noted by the Canadian government agency involved the manipulation of equipment controlling temperature and humidity in a grain-drying silo. These cases demonstrate that organizations can become victims of opportunity, even if they are not direct targets of state-affiliated adversaries.</p>
